Part two · declaring ports

## Typed declarations stay inside component code

From here on you are writing a component. Part one read ports as stored metadata. This is the typed Gleam that metadata comes from.

The task list sends a typed payload out through an output. The notes panel and the activity log each receive it through an input. Every declaration carries a stable port ID, a stable payload schema ID, and one side of the JSON codec. Gleam keeps the payload type attached while you hold the port.

{data-snippet="foundations-ports-declarations"}
```gleam
generated
```

The task list's `selected` output and the notes panel's `focus_subject` input both carry a `String`. Both name the same `watershed/task-id@1` schema. The compiler rejects a direct connection to `Input(Int)`. `port.connect` then checks the schema IDs. That catches two string-shaped payloads that mean different things, such as a task ID and a line of activity text.

{data-snippet="foundations-ports-connect"}
```gleam
generated
```
:::

::: g-block
## Erase payload types before storage

The connections between components outlive the session. They have to be stored, and stored data has no Gleam types. This is where the typed value above becomes the erased metadata part one read. `output_descriptor` and `input_descriptor` remove the payload type. They retain the port ID, direction, schema ID, and input class. A mixed component catalog lists this metadata, and a stored graph validates it. The typed input keeps the decoder a host uses when it executes a delivery.

{data-source-label="component port flow" data-caption="authors keep payload types; hosts store metadata"}
```text
typed component code             erased host boundary

Output(String) -- encode --> Json
      |                         |
      | output_descriptor       | PortRef + connection ID
      v                         v
Descriptor ---------> EffectiveGraph ---------> Delivery
                                                   |
                         target Input(String) <----+
                                decode + execute: component runtime
```

A component descriptor carries that erased metadata for every port it declares. `component.ports` is what a host reads to list the notes panel's inputs or to check a stored edge against it, with no payload type in sight.

{data-snippet="foundations-components-ports"}
```gleam
generated
```

The typed values stay inside component code, and they are what actually crosses the boundary. `component.emit` runs the output's codec before the payload leaves. `component.deliver` finds the handler that matches the delivered port ID and decodes before component code sees anything.

{data-snippet="foundations-components-deliver"}
```gleam
generated
```

Types protect code written together. Descriptors let the host validate data it reads back later — which is every check part one made.
:::

::: g-block
### The browser runtime follows the whole trace

Watershed's JavaScript component runtime takes the plan from data to execution. For each delivery it:

- decodes the target payload;
- calls the typed component handler;
- stores the handler's next running value;
- queues any outputs the handler emitted;
- remembers which edges this trace already crossed.

A local input changes only the origin's controller state. A collaborative input submits once on the origin. Ordinary channel replication carries the mutation to peers without sending it through the graph again. [The workspace layer](/component-model/workspaces) gives the runtime the persisted manifest and graph it keeps reconciled.
:::

::: g-out
{.annot}
The route

Gleam checks payload types for direct connections. After type erasure, the host checks schema IDs and descriptors. The effective graph turns an invalid stored edge set into one deterministic acyclic view. Dispatch plans ordered deliveries for local intent and suppresses a replicated echo. Next: [how a workspace keeps instances, order, and wiring in one document without throwing unfamiliar data away](/component-model/workspaces).
:::
